	// --- /proc/net/snmp: the TCP and UDP MIBs, minus their error counters ---
	//
	// These seven land in host_proto_samples, a THIRD host-level table, for
	// exactly the reason the seventy above land in host_snmp_samples: a
	// continuous aggregate cannot gain a column, so adding these to either
	// existing table would drop and recreate its rollups and destroy everything
	// older than the seven days of raw still on disk. host_snmp_samples is not
	// the answer either -- it is an existing aggregate, and widening it costs
	// the IP and ICMP history that table exists to hold.
	//
	// The error counters they belong beside (tcp_retrans_segs_per_s,
	// udp_in_errors_per_s and the rest) stay in host_samples where they already
	// are. Splitting one MIB across two tables reads wrong and IS wrong-looking,
	// but moving a stored column costs the same rollup rebuild as adding one,
	// and the UI joins across families anyway -- see the fragmentation panels.
	//
	// There is no tcp6_* mirror, for the reason netstat.go documents: Linux's
	// Tcp: block is family-agnostic and /proc/net/snmp6 has no Tcp6 block.
